# ⚠️ THIS FILE EXISTS BECAUSE OF ONE SPECIFIC FAILURE, 10 Aug 2026.
#
# `scripts/netlify-ignore.mjs` is imported by `tests/netlify-ignore.test.js`.
# On Windows git checked it out with CRLF endings, and esbuild — which is what
# Vitest transforms with — strips a `#!/usr/bin/env node` shebang up to the
# newline and leaves the `\r` behind. That is not a valid token, so the file
# failed to parse and the suite reported:
#
#     SyntaxError: Invalid or unexpected token
#     ❯ tests/netlify-ignore.test.js:7:31
#
# pointing at the IMPORT LINE IN THE TEST, several files away from the cause,
# on a line that is blank.
#
# ⚠️ THE PART THAT MAKES IT WORTH A FILE: IT IS INVISIBLE IN CI. GitHub Actions
# runs on Linux and checks out LF, so `test` and `docs-check` both stay green
# while the suite fails on BOTH of Jay's PCs. That is the mirror image of the
# usual trap — normally CI is the honest one — and it means a green pull request
# would be no evidence at all.
#
# These are Unix scripts: Netlify runs them on Linux, GitHub Actions runs them on
# Linux, and nothing about them wants CRLF on any platform. Pinning them to LF
# removes the whole class rather than the one instance — `session-guard.mjs` and
# `docs-check.mjs` keep their shebangs and are currently safe only because no
# test imports them, which is luck rather than design.
*.mjs text eol=lf

# The same reasoning for the SQL that gets pasted into the Supabase editor and
# for shell scripts, neither of which has any reason to carry CRLF.
*.sql text eol=lf
*.sh  text eol=lf

# ⚠️ WIDENED TO EVERYTHING, 24 Aug 2026 — the `.gitattributes` decision from
# the 21 Aug review (claude/open-items.md). The system `core.autocrlf=true`
# turned a 78-line test change into a 526-line diff by flattening a CRLF
# working-tree file on `git add`, and the three patterns above only protected
# the file types that had already been bitten.
#
# MEASURED BEFORE WIDENING, not assumed: `git ls-files --eol` showed every
# tracked text file already LF in the index (645 i/lf w/crlf + 215 i/lf w/lf,
# 18 binary, zero i/crlf) — so this line renormalizes NOTHING and no churn
# commit is needed. It only pins what is already true, for every future file
# and every future checkout. No .bat/.cmd/.ps1 in the repo wants CRLF —
# checked. The specific rules above are kept: they are the record of which
# types actually bit, and deleting a rot-anchor is against the rules.
* text=auto eol=lf
